1. Light / dark (and custom) app icons



What the user sees

Settings → Appearance → icon style:





Default = light-mode icons (MacTahoe / peachOS curated / peachos-icon-watcherd masks)



Dark = hand-authored dark tiles for apps we ship art for; everything else stays light



Custom = per-app Default / Dark / uploaded file (Manage Custom Icons)

This is not a GTK theme swap and not auto-tinted light icons. Dark mode only uses real curated files.

How switching is implemented

Tool: apps/iconmasker/peachos-icon-appearance

peachos-icon-appearance <default|dark|custom>

Must be invoked with python, not as a bare executable. User-local copies are often mode 644. Settings does:

[sys.executable, ICON_APPEARANCE_SCRIPT, style_id]

See icon_appearance_argv() in apps/settings/src/appearance_page.py.

User-local overrides only — never rewrite /usr/share/applications

Each styled app gets:





~/.local/share/applications/<id>.desktop — copy of the system launcher with only Icon= changed, plus marker X-PeachOS-AppearanceOverride=true



~/.local/share/icons/peachos-dark/<slug>.svg (or .png) — copy of curated dark art

XDG already prefers ~/.local/share/applications over /usr/share. The system .desktop files stay untouched so they don’t fight peachos-icon-watcherd (the light-mode masking daemon) and so GNOME’s app-picker-layout doesn’t get corrupted by a burst of writes to files Shell watches.

Default = delete our override files (or restore a .peachos-original backup). The original system launcher (light-masked) becomes visible again. No image processing.

Switching dark → custom or custom → dark always runs apply_default() first, then the new style. apply_custom() only writes apps in the manifest; without the reset, leftover Dark overrides would stick.

How an app is matched to dark art

apps/iconmasker/peachos_icon_resolve.py:





resolve_dark_for_app(desktop_path, icon, name) tokens: Name=, desktop id, Icon= basename



find_dark_svg(slug) walks, in order:





~/.local/share/icons/peachos-darkmode-src/



/usr/share/icons/peachos-darkmode-src/



repo assets/app-icons/darkmode/ and assets/app-icons/presets/darkmode/



CURATED_DARK_SLUGS maps known light icon files (Mail, Notes, Messages, Calendar, presets, …) to those slugs



Third-party apps: apps/iconmasker/peachos_icon_presets_registry.py. Every preset has assets/app-icons/presets/darkmode/<slug>.svg even if it is a byte-identical copy of light. That is the rule: “no separate dark art” means pixel-identical, not “run the auto-dark generator.”

The watcher must not remask these dirs. OWN_DIRS in peachos_icon_resolve.py includes peachos-dark, peachos-darkmode-src, peachos-presets, icloud-for-linux, etc. If a dark Icon= path is treated as uncurated, the watcher overwrites it with a light mask within a few seconds. That was a real bug.

Enumeration pitfalls (already fixed — do not reintroduce)

_enumerate_candidates() in peachos-icon-appearance:





Scan order: /usr/share/applications, snap, Flatpak exports, then ~/.local/share/applications



Dedup by desktop filename, first-seen wins (matches $XDG_DATA_DIRS)



Do not mark a name “seen” if the file was skipped (NoDisplay, not Type=Application). Otherwise a hidden apt stub (Text Editor) blocks the real Flatpak launcher



Flatpak apps must be scanned (/var/lib/flatpak/exports/share/applications). Missing that meant Dark mode silently did nothing for Calendar / Weather / etc.



Permanent user launchers (Calendar’s iCloud icon, immutable Telegram/Discord .desktop with chattr +i) are snapshotted as <file>.peachos-original before we stamp the override marker. apply_default() restores the backup instead of deleting into a generic Flatpak icon. Skip chattr -i on Telegram/Discord unless the user asks



_write_override() must not abort the whole run if one file is immutable; log and continue or the UI can show Default while half the dock is already dark



Adding a new dark icon





Drop art in assets/app-icons/darkmode/<slug>.svg or presets/ + presets/darkmode/



Register the slug in CURATED_DARK_SLUGS and/or PRESETS



Copy to live ~/.local/share/icons/peachos-darkmode-src/ if testing without provision



Re-run peachos-icon-appearance dark





2. Dock apps shuffling when icons change



Root cause

peachos-icon-appearance rewrites many .desktop files in one burst. GNOME dash.js _redisplay() assumes only one item moved at a time. If several GDesktopAppInfos re-resolve in the same second, it “remove[s] all the launchers and add[s] them back in a new order.”

peachOS dock (macos-dock-2026 / live macos-dock-2.00@local) wraps that dash and rebuilds from dash._box child order on installed-changed. So a style switch reshuffles every pinned app.

org.gnome.shell favorite-apps is not a complete order: running-but-unpinned apps are in the dock and missing from that list. Restoring only favorites drops those apps and they land wherever _appChildren() enumerates them.

What exists on the bus (older freeze)

extensions/macos-top-panel@local.dev/lib/dockOrderGuard.js





Session name: org.peachos.DockOrderGuard



Path: /org/peachos/DockOrderGuard



Methods: Snapshot(), Restore()



Constructed from macos-top-panel@local.dev/extension.js (must stay enabled)



Snapshot(): record each dash child’s app.get_id(), replace dash._queueRedisplay with a no-op



Restore(): put _queueRedisplay back, box.set_child_at_index to the snapshot (not favorites), call dock _beginAnimation(), then 200ms later apply the snapshot again because the real _redisplay() is scheduled with Meta.later_add and can still reorder unpinned running apps



Do not null _snapshot on Restore. Settings used to call Restore twice; the second call fell through to favorite-apps and shuffled unpinned apps



Safety unfreeze: 45s if Settings dies mid-swap

Current Settings does not call Guard Restore. Comments in appearance_page.py: Restore’s _queueRedisplay() is the scramble. The live swap uses dock msg-to-ext instead (next section). Leave the Guard in the top panel anyway — it is the in-process freeze if something else still Snapshots, and it documents the dash.js failure mode.

What Settings does now (the path that actually runs)

run_icon_appearance() in apps/settings/src/appearance_page.py:





Snapshot app-picker-layout and favorite-apps (GSettings), restore them after the subprocess even if dash actors stayed put



_eval_dock(_DOCK_IA_BEGIN_JS) — freeze + hide (below)



Wait ~420ms for the slide-out



Run peachos-icon-appearance



Put GSettings layout/favorites back



_eval_dock(_DOCK_IA_FINISH_JS) — wait until live actors’ gicons match the new .desktop files, pin order, then slide in



Poll $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/peachos-ia-dock until it reads ready (or 10s timeout)

_eval_dock writes JS to the running dock schema key msg-to-ext (org.gnome.shell.extensions.macos-dock). Schema dirs tried, in order: repo UUID, macos-dock-2.00@local, dash2dock-lite.

Order pinning inside the dock eval

The injected object is this._peachIA on the dock extension (this = extension instance):





snapshot(d) → d._peachOrder = app.get_id() list from d.dash._box (includes unpinned running). Also snapshotRunning(d) → d._peachRunning window counts for the running dots.



freeze(d) → no-op _queueRedisplay and wrap _redisplay (see “shuffle then revert” below)



applyOrder(d) → set_child_at_index from _peachOrder, place the favorites|running separator after the last pinned app, never treat _extraIcons as a ghost, pin Trash/Downloads last, d._icons = null



refreshIcons(d) → push new gicons onto existing actors from ~/.local/share/applications/<id>.desktop



After reveal: armPin restores real _queueRedisplay so unpinned running apps can rematch, but _redisplay stays wrapped so pinOrder runs in the same turn



keepPinned() re-applies every 350ms for 12 beats, then disarmPin after 20s (unwrap _redisplay). Do not null _peachRunning on disarm — AppSystem briefly reports 0 windows after a .desktop burst and that blanks the dots.

Do not “fix order after a visible flicker.” Freeze before writes, keep _box stable, push new gicons onto existing actors, then show.





2b. Dock bugs we actually hit (and how they were fixed)

These all showed up on the live laptop while switching Default/Dark with apps running. Do not “simplify” them away.

Shuffle, then it snaps back (visible flicker)

What it looked like: icons jump into a random order for a moment, then revert to the right order.

Why: dash._queueRedisplay() does not rebuild synchronously. It queues Meta.later_add / deferred work; the real scramble is dash._redisplay(). An older pin did:





call original _queueRedisplay (schedules scramble)



GLib.timeout_add(..., 220, pinOrder) — restore after that frame had already painted

That 220ms delay is the flicker. DockOrderGuard had the same pattern (POST_REDISPLAY_REAPPLY_MS).

Fix: wrap _redisplay itself:

dash._peachOrigRedisplay = proto._redisplay.bind(dash);
dash._redisplay = function(){
  origR.apply(dash, arguments);
  if (d._peachPinActive) pinOrder(d);
};

pinOrder (applyOrder + refreshIcons + _beginAnimation) runs before Clutter paints. No timeout. _peachPinActive is set in freeze / armPin / thawOnce and cleared in disarmPin.

Do not go back to “queue orig, wait 200–220ms, applyOrder.” That will always flash.

Ugly glass hole / Trash tray collapsed after a dark swap

What it looked like: extra gap in the glass before Trash, or Trash/Downloads sitting on width-0 space, dock “acting weird” with unpinned running apps.

Why (two layers):





dock.js _findIcons() used to remove_child every dash-separator from dash._box. Trash/Downloads live in _extraIcons, which is itself a child of _box, and _extraIcons has its own separator. Wiping box separators left a hole after a swap. Fix in dock.js: only extra box separators, never _extraIcons children; do not remove_child the extras container.



applyOrder treated any _box child with no app id as a ghost (visible=false, width=0). _extraIcons has no app id → Trash/Downloads container forced to width 0. Confirmed live: extraW=0 while children were still 56px. Animator still drew them, glass/layout did not.

Fix in applyOrder: skip d._extraIcons when collecting ghosts; after placing apps, restore extra.visible = true, width/height = -1, set_child_at_index(extra, last).

Running dots disappear on icon swap

After the .desktop burst, getAppWindowsFiltered / get_n_windows can briefly return 0. animator.js now falls back to dock._peachRunning[appId] (filled by snapshotRunning, refreshed in pinOrder). Do not d._peachRunning = null in disarmPin.

Unpinned running apps also need thawOnce (one real _queueRedisplay while still hidden) so Dash rematches them; freeze-only left them stuck on old actors / missing from the running section. thawOnce still wraps _redisplay so that one rebuild does not paint a shuffle.

Favorites | running separator in the wrong place

applyOrder used to pack every app to the front and leave the GNOME dash-separator at the end of _box (empty glass before Trash). It now sits at lastFav + 1 when unpinned running apps exist, and is hidden otherwise.

About This PC showing as a dock app

The About window is a separate gjs -m aboutWindow.js process (com.github.kemma.KiwiMenu.About). Window tracker often tags it as GJS, not KiwiMenu, so an id-prefix filter misses it.

Fix: dashFilter.js + dock _inspectIcon hide by window title About This PC / peachySearch and wm_class containing kiwimenu. aboutWindow.js sets GLib.set_prgname('com.github.kemma.KiwiMenu.About') and set_application_name('About This PC'). Live overlay + msg-to-ext wrap of get_running — disk JS will not load until logout.

Maximized window gap under the dock

Work-area gap is dock struts (affectsStruts). Extra iconSize * 0.2 left a wallpaper strip. Live animator.js: strut so the dock overlaps the maximized window by 1px (WINDOW_OVERLAP = 1). Apply via msg-to-ext / overlay; ESM cache until logout.

Notification badges

Cairo Dot overlays. Size iconW * 0.368, position x = renderer.x + iconW - D * 0.94, y = renderer.y - D * 0.08. Font SF Pro Rounded Medium (drawing.js draw_text_ink_centered). Inter Heavy was not installed.

OBS does not record the dock

The dock is GNOME Shell chrome (dash actors are opacity 0; the visible plate is renderArea). Window Capture never sees it — use Display Capture. Autohide / window-dodge also slides the dock away when OBS is maximized, so it will not be in the recording. Not an icon-swap bug.





3. Hiding the dock while icons change

Same _peachIA.begin() / .finish() / .abort() blob in appearance_page.py.

Begin (hide)





Write $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/peachos-ia-dock = pending



self._iconAppearanceHold = true



Force autohide_dash = false and disable each dock’s autohider so autohide doesn’t fight the animation



Clear icon_map_cache / app_map_cache



For each dock: freeze redisplay, replace slideIn with a no-op (holdSlide), snapshot order, slideOut()

The no-op slideIn matters: GNOME/autohide will try to bring the dock back while .desktop files are still changing. Hold it off-screen until icons actually match.

Finish (show only when icons are really new)

Do not slide in when the subprocess exits. Dash actors still show old gicons for a bit.

Poll every 120ms:





refreshIcons(d) — reload Gio.DesktopAppInfo from ~/.local/share/applications/<id> first, then Gio.DesktopAppInfo.new(id), assign gicon onto c._icon, c._grid, c._renderer, c.child.icon



applyOrder(d)



iconsMatch(d) — compare path/names of wanted vs current gicon; require at least 4 checked icons

Ready when iconsMatch succeeds 8 consecutive polls and at least 18 tries, or 70 tries (timeout). Then: apply order, _icons = null, restore real slideIn and call it, armPin (real _queueRedisplay + _redisplay wrap, not a 220ms timeout), restore autohide, keepPinned(), _iconAppearanceHold = false, write state ready.

Abort

If peachos-icon-appearance fails: restore order, reveal, disarm pin, restore autohide, write ready, un-busy the Appearance page.

